  "verbalization": "Phomalide appears to have a direct relationship with MIP through a series of molecular targets, with SNU-387 acting as a common intermediary. The evidence suggests that Phomalide targets SNU-387, which in turn is a target of multiple compounds, including S-ethylcysteine zwitterion and 5-hydroxy-6,8-dimethoxy-2,3-dimethylbenzo[G]chromen-4-one. All these compounds ultimately have a shared target, MIP. This indicates that Phomalide may interact with MIP through a common pathway, possibly by modulating the activity of SNU-387, which is involved in multiple downstream processes. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism where Phomalide influences MIP via a shared target, possibly through a regulatory or metabolic interaction. The evidence supports a role for Phomalide in modulating the activity of SNU-387, which in turn affects MIP, suggesting a broader pharmacological impact.",
